.\" fast-highlight(1): the fast-highlight command.
.TH FAST-HIGHLIGHT 1 2026-10-04 "fast-highlight 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
fast-highlight \- syntax highlighter for the zsh line editor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fast-highlight serve
.RB [ \-\-timing ]
.RB [ \-\-log
.IR path ]
.RB [ \-\-parent
.IR pid ]
.br
.B fast-highlight styles
.br
.B fast-highlight highlight
.RB [ \-\-cwd
.IR dir ]
.RB [ \-\-opts
.IR letters ]
.RB [ \-\-timing ]
.RB [ \-\-repeat
.IR n ]
.RB [ \-\-format
.BR spans | ansi ]
.RB [ \-\- ]
.RI [ text " ...]"
.br
.B fast-highlight check-config
.br
.B fast-highlight plugin-path
.br
.B fast-highlight help
|
.B \-\-help
|
.B \-h
.br
.B fast-highlight \-\-version
|
.B \-V
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B fast-highlight
highlights the command line of the zsh line editor.
A zsh plugin starts one
.B fast-highlight serve
process per interactive shell and keeps it running for the life of the shell.
Before each redraw of the line, the plugin sends the edit buffer to the daemon, which parses it
and returns a list of spans, each a range of the buffer and a token type such as
.BR command ,
.BR path ,
or
.BR error .
The daemon returns token types, not styles.
The plugin maps each token type to a zsh highlight style through its style table and fills
.BR region_highlight .
.PP
The daemon checks command names against the shell's aliases, functions, builtins, and reserved
words, which the plugin sends, and against the commands in
.BR PATH ,
which it scans itself.
It checks paths on disk, recognises the subcommands and options of commands that have a
command spec, and marks syntax errors and unknown commands with the
.B error
token type.
.PP
The other commands compile the theme into the plugin's style table, highlight text outside the
shell for testing and timing, validate the configuration, and locate the plugin file.
The configuration, theme, and command spec file formats are described in
.BR fast-highlight (5).
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B serve
Runs the daemon.
It reads request frames on standard input and writes response frames on standard output, as
described under
.BR PROTOCOL .
The zsh plugin starts it; it is not normally run by hand.
.IP
The daemon reads
.B config.toml
and the command specs once, at startup.
A change to either takes effect when the daemon restarts, which
.B fasthl-reload
does.
When
.B config.toml
fails to load, the daemon uses the default configuration and writes the error to the log.
.IP
Nothing but response frames is written to standard output, and nothing is written to standard
error.
Diagnostics go to the log file described under
.BR LOGGING .
The daemon ignores
.B SIGINT
and
.BR SIGPIPE ,
so a Ctrl-C sent to the shell's process group does not stop it, and a write to a closed pipe
fails instead of killing it.
.IP
The daemon exits when it receives a
.B Q
request, when standard input reaches end of file, when it meets a framing error, when reading or
writing fails, or when its parent process exits.
The parent process counts as exited once the daemon's parent process ID differs from the one it
had at startup.
The daemon checks this at least once per second while its input is idle.
.IP
The daemon changes its working directory to
.BR / ,
so it does not keep the file system it started on busy.
It resolves relative paths against the directory it started in until a request names another.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-timing
Writes one timing line per request to the log.
The
.B timing
key in the
.B [log]
table of
.B config.toml
has the same effect.
.TP
.BI \-\-log " path"
Writes the log to
.IR path ,
overriding the
.B file
key in the
.B [log]
table of
.B config.toml
and the default location.
A relative
.I path
is resolved against the directory the daemon started in.
.TP
.BI \-\-parent " pid"
Watches the process
.I pid
in addition to the parent process, and exits once no process with that ID exists.
.I pid
is a positive integer.
A process that exists but that the daemon has no permission to signal counts as existing.
The check runs at least once per second while the input is idle.
The plugin passes the shell's process ID, so the daemon also exits when the shell dies before
the daemon has recorded its parent, a case in which the recorded parent is already the process
that adopted the daemon.
.RE
.TP
.B styles
Prints the active theme as zsh code that defines the global associative array
.BR FASTHL_STYLES ,
one token type per line, sorted by name, each style single-quoted.
Token types with no style are omitted.
The plugin evaluates this output when it loads and when
.B fasthl-reload
runs.
.IP
When
.B config.toml
fails to load,
.B styles
reports the error on standard error and continues with the default configuration.
When the theme fails to load, it reports the error and prints the built-in
.B truecolor
theme.
Either failure makes the exit status 1.
.TP
.B highlight
Highlights
.I text
and prints one line per span:
.RS
.IP
.IR "start end kind" " \(dq" text \(dq
.RE
.IP
.I start
and
.I end
are a half-open range in the units selected by
.BR \-\-opts ,
.I kind
is the token type, and the final field is the text the span covers, double-quoted with backslash
escapes.
The
.I text
arguments are joined by single spaces.
With no
.I text
argument,
.B highlight
reads standard input and removes one final newline.
A word that starts with
.B \-\-
and is not an option of
.B highlight
is a usage error unless it follows
.BR \-\- .
.B \-\-help
or
.B \-h
before the first
.I text
argument prints the help.
.IP
.B highlight
loads
.B config.toml
and the command specs as the daemon does, and reports config errors and spec warnings on
standard error without changing the exit status.
It knows zsh's builtins and reserved words and the commands in its own
.BR PATH ,
but no aliases, functions, or named directories, because no shell sends it any.
Text longer than the
.B hard-cap-bytes
limit produces no spans.
.RS
.TP
.BI \-\-cwd " dir"
Resolves relative paths against
.IR dir .
The default is the current directory.
.TP
.BI \-\-opts " letters"
Sets the option letters of the highlight request, as in the
.B opt
field of the protocol:
.B u
for offsets in characters (bytes when absent),
.B c
for
.BR INTERACTIVE_COMMENTS ,
.B a
for
.BR AUTO_CD ,
.B e
for
.BR EXTENDED_GLOB ,
.B k
for
.BR KSH_GLOB ,
.B b
for
.BR IGNORE_BRACES ,
.B B
for
.BR IGNORE_CLOSE_BRACES ,
.B r
for
.BR RC_QUOTES ,
.B K
for
.BR KSH_ARRAYS ,
.B p
for
.BR POSIX_IDENTIFIERS ,
.B s
for
.BR SH_GLOB ,
.B C
for
.BR BRACE_CCL ,
.B E
for
.BR NO_EQUALS ,
and
.B L
for
.BR NO_SHORT_LOOPS .
Without a letter, the option is in its zsh default state.
Unknown letters are ignored.
The default is
.BR u .
.TP
.B \-\-timing
Prints a summary of the processing time over all runs to standard error, in the form
.RS
.IP
.BI runs= n " min=" t "us median=" t "us max=" t us
.RE
.IP
with times in microseconds.
.TP
.BI \-\-repeat " n"
Highlights the text
.I n
times, for
.BR \-\-timing .
.I n
is a positive integer; the default is 1.
.TP
.BR "\-\-format spans" | ansi
Selects the output format.
.B spans
is the default and prints the span lines described above.
.B ansi
prints the text once, coloured with ANSI escape sequences from the active theme, followed by a
reset sequence and a newline.
This format always uses character offsets, adding
.B u
to the option letters.
It renders
.BR fg= ,
.BR bg= ,
.BR bold ,
.BR underline ,
.BR standout ,
and
.BR none .
A three-digit
.BI # rgb
colour is rendered as
.BI # rrggbb ,
as zsh reads it.
.TP
.B \-\-
Ends option parsing; every later argument is
.IR text .
.RE
.TP
.B check-config
Prints the config directory, then loads
.BR config.toml ,
the theme, and the command specs and reports problems.
Errors in the config file and the theme are printed on standard error as lines starting with
.BR error: ,
and spec files that fail to load as lines starting with
.BR "warning: specs:" .
The last line on standard output is
.B ok
when there are no problems, and otherwise the number of errors and warnings.
The exit status is 1 when there is at least one error.
Spec warnings alone leave it 0, because the daemon skips a broken spec file and loads the rest.
.TP
.B plugin-path
Prints the path of the zsh plugin file,
.BR fast-highlight.plugin.zsh .
The candidates, in order, are
.BR $FAST_HIGHLIGHT_SHARE/fast-highlight.plugin.zsh ,
.B ../share/fast-highlight/fast-highlight.plugin.zsh
relative to the directory of the running binary, and the
.B plugin
directory of the source tree the binary was built from.
The first candidate that is a regular file is printed.
When none is, a message goes to standard error and the exit status is 1.
.TP
.BR help ", " \-\-help ", " \-h
Prints a summary of the commands and options.
Any command other than
.B highlight
followed by
.B \-\-help
or
.B \-h
among its arguments does the same.
.TP
.BR \-\-version ", " \-V
Prints the version.
.SH PROTOCOL
The plugin and the daemon exchange frames over the daemon's standard input and standard output.
A frame is a header line,
.BI "FH1 " "type id length" ,
followed by a body of
.I length
bytes.
The request types are
.B H
(highlight),
.B S
(shell state),
.B P
(ping), and
.B Q
(quit).
The response types are
.B R
(result),
.B A
(acknowledgement), and
.B E
(error).
Each response carries the id of the request it answers, and the plugin discards a response whose
id is older than the request it is waiting for, so a late response is never applied to a newer
buffer.
.PP
A highlight request carries
.B BUFFER
and
.BR PREBUFFER ,
the cursor position, the current directory when it has changed, and the option letters listed
under
.BR \-\-opts .
A result body is one line per span,
.IR "start end kind" ,
in the request's offset unit.
A result holds at most
.B max-spans
spans, the first ones in order, and is empty when the text exceeds
.BR hard-cap-bytes ;
both limits are described in
.BR fast-highlight (5).
A state request carries the shell's aliases, global aliases, suffix aliases, functions, builtins,
reserved words, named directories, and
.BR PATH ,
and a
.B rehash
field after the user runs
.B rehash
or
.B hash \-r
in the shell, which makes the daemon reread every
.B PATH
directory.
A malformed named-directory list is ignored with a warning in the log, and the rest of the state
request applies.
.PP
The normative specification is
.B docs/protocol.md
in the source distribution.
.SH LOGGING
The daemon appends diagnostics to a log file: panics, a config file that failed to load, spec
files that failed to load, the reason for an exit caused by an error, and, when timing is
enabled, one line per request.
The log file is the first of:
.IP \(bu 2
the
.I path
given to
.BR \-\-log ;
.IP \(bu 2
the
.B file
key in the
.B [log]
table of
.BR config.toml ;
.IP \(bu 2
.BR $XDG_STATE_HOME/\:fast-highlight/\:fast-highlight.log ,
when
.B XDG_STATE_HOME
is set and not empty;
.IP \(bu 2
.BR $HOME/\:.local/\:state/\:fast-highlight/\:fast-highlight.log ,
when
.B HOME
is set and not empty.
.PP
When none applies, nothing is logged.
Missing parent directories are created.
Each line starts with the Unix time in seconds with a three-digit fractional part.
A timing line has the form
.PP
.RS
.BI id= n " type=" t " bytes=" n " spans=" n " us=" n
.RE
.PP
where
.B type
is the request type
.RB ( H ,
.BR S ,
or
.BR P ),
.B bytes
is the size of
.B PREBUFFER
and
.B BUFFER
together for a highlight request and 0 otherwise, and
.B us
is the processing time in microseconds.
A failure to write the log is ignored.
.SH ZSH INTEGRATION
The plugin is
.BR fast-highlight.plugin.zsh ,
sourced from
.BR ~/.zshrc .
Its position relative to other plugins does not matter.
It requires zsh 5.8 or later, and does nothing in a non-interactive shell, in a shell whose
standard input is not a terminal, or under
.B zsh \-c
or a script.
zsh-syntax-highlighting and fast-syntax-highlighting both rewrite
.B region_highlight
on every redraw, so loading either in the same shell conflicts with the plugin.
.PP
The plugin starts the daemon as
.B fast-highlight serve \-\-parent
.I pid
followed by the elements of
.BR FASTHL_SERVE_ARGS ,
with standard error sent to
.BR /dev/null .
When the daemon is missing, fails, or misses the timeout, that redraw has no highlighting and the
daemon is stopped.
The plugin starts it again on a later hook after a backoff that begins at 0.5 seconds and doubles
up to 60 seconds; a successful highlight resets it.
Each redraw waits at most
.B FASTHL_TIMEOUT
seconds for the daemon, and the plugin prints nothing to the terminal.
.SS Shell parameters
All are optional.
.TP
.B FASTHL_BIN
The path or command name of the
.B fast-highlight
binary.
When unset, the plugin uses
.B fast-highlight
from
.BR PATH ,
else
.B ../target/release/fast-highlight
relative to the plugin directory.
When no binary is found, the plugin stays inactive and prints nothing.
.TP
.B FASTHL_TIMEOUT
The number of seconds the plugin waits for the daemon on each redraw before giving up on that
redraw and restarting the daemon.
A value that is not a non-negative decimal number is ignored.
The default is 0.05.
.TP
.B FASTHL_STYLES
An associative array that maps token types to
.B region_highlight
styles such as
.BR fg=green,bold .
The plugin fills it from
.B fast-highlight styles
when it loads, whatever its exit status, so a broken theme or config file yields the theme that
.B styles
falls back to.
Only when that command prints no table does the plugin use a built-in table of its own, which
matches the built-in
.B truecolor
theme.
An entry assigned after the plugin is sourced overrides the theme and survives
.BR fasthl-reload .
An empty style leaves the token type unstyled.
.TP
.B FASTHL_SERVE_ARGS
An array of extra arguments for
.BR "fast-highlight serve" ,
such as
.BR "(\-\-timing)" .
It is read each time the daemon starts.
.TP
.B FASTHL_MAX_LENGTH
The most bytes of
.B PREBUFFER
followed by
.B BUFFER
the plugin sends to the daemon.
A longer command line is not sent and gets no highlighting.
A value that is not a decimal integer of at most 15 digits is ignored.
The default is 65536, the default of
.B hard-cap-bytes
in
.BR fast-highlight (5);
raising one has no effect without raising the other.
.SS Shell functions
.TP
.B fasthl-reload
Reads the theme into
.B FASTHL_STYLES
again, keeping entries the user has set, and restarts the daemon, which then receives the full
shell state again.
.TP
.B fasthl-disable
Stops the daemon, removes the highlighting, and removes the plugin's hooks.
.TP
.B fasthl-enable
Reverses
.BR fasthl-disable ,
searching for the binary again.
When no binary is found, it prints a message on standard error and returns 1.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
For
.BR serve ,
a
.B Q
request or end of file on standard input.
.TP
.B 1
For
.BR serve ,
a framing error, a read or write error, or the exit of the parent process or of the process given
to
.BR \-\-parent .
For
.BR styles ,
a config file or theme that failed to load.
For
.BR highlight ,
a failure to read standard input or an internal error.
For
.BR check-config ,
at least one error.
For
.BR plugin-path ,
no plugin file found.
.TP
.B 2
A usage error: a missing or unknown command, an unknown option, a missing or invalid option
value, or an unexpected argument.
The message and the usage summary go to standard error.
.PP
Every command other than
.B serve
ends quietly, by
.BR SIGPIPE ,
when its standard output is a closed pipe.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B FAST_HIGHLIGHT_CONFIG_DIR
The config directory, when set and not empty.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
When
.B FAST_HIGHLIGHT_CONFIG_DIR
is unset or empty, the config directory is
.B $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/fast-highlight
if this variable is set to an absolute path.
.TP
.B HOME
When neither variable above applies, the config directory is
.BR $HOME/.config/fast-highlight .
For the config directory and for
.B \(ti
in the
.B file
key of
.BR config.toml ,
an unset or empty
.B HOME
is replaced by the home directory in the password database, or by
.B /
when that has none.
.B HOME
also locates the default log file when
.B XDG_STATE_HOME
is unset or empty, and is the expansion of
.B \(ti
in highlighted words.
.TP
.B XDG_STATE_HOME
The default log file is
.B $XDG_STATE_HOME/fast-highlight/fast-highlight.log
when this variable is set and not empty.
.TP
.B FAST_HIGHLIGHT_SHARE
The first directory
.B plugin-path
searches for the plugin file.
.TP
.B PATH
The daemon scans the directories in its own
.B PATH
at startup and replaces the list with the
.B PATH
of the shell when the plugin sends it.
Every entry of a listed directory that is not a directory counts as a command, as in zsh with
.B HASH_EXECUTABLES_ONLY
unset; permissions are not checked.
The daemon caches each directory's names with its modification time and, between requests and at
most once per second, rereads the directories whose modification time has changed.
.B highlight
uses its own
.BR PATH .
.PP
The plugin's shell parameters are described under
.BR "ZSH INTEGRATION" .
.SH FILES
The config directory is written
.I config
below; see
.B ENVIRONMENT
for how it is chosen.
.TP
.IB config /config.toml
Limits, log settings, and the theme name.
.TP
.IB config /theme.toml
The theme when
.B config.toml
names none.
.TP
.IB config /themes/ name .toml
The theme named
.I name
in
.BR config.toml .
.TP
.IB config /specs/*.toml
User command specs.
.TP
.B ${XDG_STATE_HOME:\-$HOME/.local/state}/fast-highlight/fast-highlight.log
The default log file.
.TP
.B fast-highlight.plugin.zsh
The zsh plugin, which sources
.B fasthl-core.zsh
from its own directory.
.PP
The formats of the files in the config directory are described in
.BR fast-highlight (5).
.SH EXAMPLES
Load the plugin from
.BR ~/.zshrc :
.PP
.RS
.EX
source "$(fast\-highlight plugin\-path)"
.EE
.RE
.PP
Highlight a command line:
.PP
.RS
.EX
$ fast\-highlight highlight \(aqls \-l /tmp | wc \-l\(aq
0 2 command "ls"
6 10 path\-directory "/tmp"
11 12 separator "|"
13 15 command "wc"
.EE
.RE
.PP
Measure the processing time of one buffer over 1000 runs:
.PP
.RS
.EX
$ fast\-highlight highlight \-\-timing \-\-repeat 1000 \(aqgit status\(aq >/dev/null
runs=1000 min=0.4us median=0.4us max=9.1us
.EE
.RE
.PP
Show a buffer in the theme's colours:
.PP
.RS
.EX
$ echo \(aqgit commit \-m "$msg"\(aq | fast\-highlight highlight \-\-format ansi
.EE
.RE
.PP
Log per-request timing from the daemon of the current shell, and override one style, in
.BR ~/.zshrc :
.PP
.RS
.EX
FASTHL_SERVE_ARGS=(\-\-timing)
source "$(fast\-highlight plugin\-path)"
FASTHL_STYLES[comment]=\(aqfg=244\(aq
.EE
.RE
.PP
Check the configuration after editing it:
.PP
.RS
.EX
$ fast\-highlight check\-config
config directory: /home/user/.config/fast\-highlight
ok
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fast-highlight (5),
.BR zsh (1),
.BR zshzle (1)
.PP
The source repository is
.UR https://github.com/wolpert/fast-highlight
.UE .
